# slashevents.io
Never lose a webhook.

- receive webhooks from various 3rd parties and store them
- provide an RPC API for the consumer service to (long) poll for new webhooks
  - getEvents(afterId, limit, timeout, filter) -> [event1, event2, ...]
    - afterId skip over events earlier than this id
    - limit max number of events to return
    - timeout max time to wait for new events
    - filter optional filter to apply to the events, e.g. only specific sources
  - no "on-board" retention policy, events are stored indefinitely, only deleted via something like `apiRpc.deleteEvent(eventId)`

## Architecture

Everything is in this one server to build the MVP. This server will host multiple components:
- appRPC: the RPC endpoint and handlers that serve the user-facing UI
  - this includes: signing up to the service, creating accounts, managing user permissions, accessible w/ JWTs
- adminRpc: the RPC endpiont and handlers that serve administrative UI
  - see all accounts and users, customer support will work with this UI, accessible w/ JWTs scoped for internal
- apiRpc: the RPC endpoints and handler to be used by the users' system
  - query/fetch events for an account, delete events, accessible with API keys
- ingress
  - endpoint for 3rd parties to send webhooks to. e.g. `<slashevents.io>/ingress/:account_uuid/:rest_of_url`
- UI (App & Admin & PDF are desktop only, Landing & Email are mobile-friendly)
  - landing page
  - "app UI" public facing UI, uses the appRPCs
  - "admin UI" administrative UI to be used by customer support, etc. Uses the adminRPCs
  - "pdf UI" internal UI to render PDFs. ONLY AVAILABLE INTERNALLY (Pupeteer w/ headless chrome opens these pages and prints to PDF (or similar)) e.g. invoices
  - "email UI" similar to PDF UI but for html email body rendering. ONLY AVAILABLE INTERNALLY

`src/server/index.ts` exports a server type that the frontend can use to make RPC calls.
`src/lib/rpc.ts` imports the server type and exports a client.

Storage:
- A postgres DB (locally running in a container. psql is available to inspect the local db)

## Environment variables

- `DATABASE_URL`: Postgres connection string
- `APP_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY` / `APP_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY`: RS256 keypair used for user session JWTs
- `API_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY` / `API_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY`: RS256 keypair used for API key JWTs (project-scoped)

## DB Tables
- Events
- Users
- Projects
- ProjectUserPermissions
- ProjectEvents
- ApiKeys

An Event belongs to a Project
Users can belong to multiple Projects
A Users' permission to a Project is defined by ProjectUserPermissions
A Project can have many ProjectEvents
An ApiKey belongs to a Project
An ApiKey's permissions are stored directly with the ApiKey

### events
- uuid PK
- project_id FK to projects.id
- http_method ENUM('GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE') NOT NULL
- url VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
- headers JSONB NOT NULL
- body TEXT
- source_ip_v4 VARCHAR(15)
- received_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP


